## Verify the download

```sh
shasum -a 256 -c dnm-@VERSION@-arm64.zip.sha256   # expects: dnm-@VERSION@-arm64.zip: OK
ditto -x -k dnm-@VERSION@-arm64.zip dnm-@VERSION@
codesign -dvv dnm-@VERSION@/dnm 2>&1 | grep -E "Authority=Developer ID|flags=.*runtime"
codesign --verify --strict --verbose=2 dnm-@VERSION@/dnm
```

The zip also holds `LICENSE`, `Acknowledgements.md` (the open-source components in `dnm`, with their
licenses) and their license texts in `Licenses/`; `dnm about` lists them too.

SHA-256 of `dnm-@VERSION@-arm64.zip`: `@SHA256@`. Changing a single byte of the zip makes the checksum
check fail. The binary is signed with a Developer ID and the hardened runtime, and notarized by Apple: a
quarantined copy (as a browser or Homebrew downloads it) runs without a Gatekeeper warning.
